Pleural Empyema
Respiratory System
Spiking fever/rigors, Localized pleuritic pain, Purulent pleural fluid

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-036
Reason for Visit: Acute clinical deterioration with high-spiking fevers, severe rigors, and worsening pleuritic chest pain requiring immediate assessment to prevent septic shock.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient recently treated for pneumonia who suddenly developed frank rigors, profound lethargy, and localized chest pain. Urgent clinical assessment was absolutely mandated to evaluate for a complicated parapneumonic effusion or empyema, as delaying care until the next available in-hours period posed a high risk of overwhelming sepsis and cardiovascular collapse.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel freezing cold but I am drenched in sweat, and breathing feels like a knife dragging through my ribs.
History of Present Illness: The patient was diagnosed with a simple lower respiratory tract infection 7 days ago. Over the past 48 hours, they have experienced profound, progressive fatigue and lethargy. Today, there was an acute onset of high-spiking fever and frank rigors. The patient describes a sharp pleuritic chest pain strictly localized to the right hemithorax and persistent, worsening dyspnea. They report severe anorexia and night sweats. No copious purulent sputum is noted currently, suggesting no bronchopleural fistula has formed yet.
Functional Status: Bedbound; completely reliant on spouse for mobility due to profound weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to systemic toxicity and rigors.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 39.4 C | RR: 26 breaths/min | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, diaphoretic, flushed, visibly shivering, and taking shallow breaths to splint the right chest wall.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals markedly decreased breath sounds and pronounced stony dullness to percussion over the right lower and middle lung zones. There is exquisite localized chest wall tenderness on palpation over the affected area. Cardiovascular exam confirms sinus tachycardia indicative of systemic inflammatory response.
Environmental Observations: Multiple heavy blankets on the bed despite the high ambient room temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with previous oral antibiotics, demonstrating definitive clinical failure.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pleural Empyema - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Uncomplicated Lobar Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of stony dullness, localized chest wall tenderness, and clinical failure despite a full week of oral antibiotic therapy.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high-spiking fevers, frank rigors, and a preceding week-long infective prodrome.

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Stony dullness to percussion rather than hyperresonance, accompanied by profound signs of systemic infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic hallmarks of a pleural empyema: clinical deterioration despite oral antibiotics, high-spiking fevers, localized pleuritic pain, and stony dullness indicating a loculated fluid collection. The borderline hypotension and significant tachycardia indicate early sepsis secondary to a walled-off intrathoracic abscess. Deferring this assessment and intervention until the next in-hours period would have predictably resulted in septic shock and potential multi-organ failure. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the presence of a complicated effusion, administer immediate intravenous fluids/temporizing broad-spectrum antibiotics, and facilitate emergency hospital admission for thoracentesis and surgical drainage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a stat dose of oral Paracetamol 1000mg and Ibuprofen 400mg to manage the rigors and pleuritic pain. Activated EMS for immediate priority ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep your right arm elevated and resting on a pillow while sitting; maintaining this specific posture physically opens the rib spaces on the affected side, directly reducing the friction against the infected lung lining and decreasing your sharp pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedics and the receiving Emergency Department physician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred via ambulance. Do not attempt to walk or stand up while awaiting the paramedics due to the high risk of fainting from low blood pressure.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The exact nature of the fluid around your lung must be confirmed with a chest CT scan and a needle sample taken at the hospital.
Clinical Course: The infection is trapped in a pocket outside the lung tissue; it requires specialized drainage tubes and intravenous antibiotics to clear completely.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Remain completely flat in bed until the ambulance arrives to protect your blood pressure and prevent a fall.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The initial pneumonia infection has leaked into the space between your lung and your ribs, creating a pocket of pus that oral antibiotics cannot reach; the hospital will insert a small tube to physically drain the trapped infection.
Actionable Steps: Take slow, controlled breaths into your belly rather than shallow chest breaths; focusing on your diaphragm physically limits the movement of your ribcage, directly preventing the sharp, stabbing pain while you wait for transport.

